## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives (`string`, `number`), change means different value. For objects and functions, change means different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// This creates a NEW object on every render of Parent
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// This creates a NEW function on every render of Parent
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of `Parent` gives `Child` a brand-new prop reference, so `Child` always re-renders — even if nothing meaningful changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component, skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
- Use when: a child is expensive to render and its props don't change often.
- Useless without also stabilizing the props passed to it.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
- Use when: passing a callback to a memoized child, or as a dependency in `useEffect`/`useMemo`.
- Don't use everywhere — the memoization itself has a cost.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value.
- Use when: the computation is genuinely expensive, or you need a stable object reference to pass as a prop.
- Not for "just in case" — profiling should justify it.

The mental model: `React.memo` is the gate; `useCallback`/`useMemo` are what make props stable enough to pass through it.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value change re-renders **every consumer**, regardless of which part of the value they use.

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every render
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Every render of `App` creates a new `{ user, setUser }` object, re-rendering all consumers.

**Fixes:**
- `useMemo` to stabilize the context value.
- Split contexts — separate frequently-changing state from stable state (e.g., `UserContext` vs `UserActionsContext`).

```jsx
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before/After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the input:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([1, 2, 3]);

  const handleDelete = (id) => {          // new reference every render
    setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i !== id));
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, onDelete }) => { /* expensive render */ };
```

Typing in the input re-renders `Parent`, which creates a new `handleDelete` reference, which re-renders `ExpensiveList` — even though `items` didn't change.

**After** — stabilize the callback, memoize the child:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([1, 2, 3]);

  const handleDelete = useCallback((id) => {
    setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i !== id));
  }, []);                                 // stable reference — no deps needed
                                          // because we use the functional updater form

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onDelete }) => { /* expensive render */ });
```

Now typing only re-renders `Parent`. `ExpensiveList` sees the same `items` and `handleDelete` references, so `React.memo` skips it entirely.

---

**Key principle:** measure first with React DevTools Profiler. Premature memoization adds complexity and can hurt performance if overused — every `useMemo`/`useCallback` runs a comparison on every render.
